<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.2.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00871</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.2.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3cf84454-272d-402e-8842-52be6b92187c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<!--Do not edit this div tag.  It must surround the interaction block-->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="9a0e8228-dbbc-42d4-9bc8-0ea042e64963" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>1. The height of water in a bathtub, \(w\), is a function of time, \(t\). Let \(P\) represent this function. Height is measured in inches and time in minutes.</para><para>What does each equation mean in this situation?</para>
      <para>\(P(0) = 0\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The bathtub starts out with no water.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.2.2. </para><para>The correct answer is the bathtub starts out with no water. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="3379ac21-fa25-4ce5-a6a6-1d8e84d30603" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(P(4) = 10\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The height of the water is 10 inches after 4 minutes.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.2.2. </para><para>The correct answer is the height of the water is 10 inches after 4 minutes. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="9026ab6b-c2b5-4f4c-b535-f7c79714eed2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(P(10) = 4\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! After 10 minutes, the height of the water is 4 inches.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.2.2. </para><para>The correct answer is the height of the water is 10 inches after 4 minutes. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="e54beaa5-fe9d-410f-a372-7c182d63121a" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(P(20) = 0\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! After 20 minutes, the bathtub is empty.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.2.2. </para><para>The correct answer is: After 20 minutes, the bathtub is empty. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="9ba0c414-9e72-40cf-8171-f1a2008ab925" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Function \(C\) takes time for its input and gives a student’s Monday class for its output.
          
          Use function notation to represent: A student has English at 10:00.
        </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(C(English)=10:00\)</para><para>\(C(10:00)=English\)</para><para>\(C(Monday)=English\) \(at\) \(10:00\)</para><para>\(C(10:00)=Monday\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(C(10:00)=English\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.2.2.</para><para>The correct answer is \(C(10:00)=English\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="db790a9a-4c23-4f64-99c6-d07ff5ce0469" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Function \(f\) gives the distance of a dog from a post, in feet, as a function of time, in seconds, since its owner left.</para>
      <figure><media alt="Nonlinear function. time in seconds and distance from post in feet.  "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/34491baf9eedf82892115acb6b6df9202640cb5e"/></media></figure><para> Find the value of \(f(20)\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(f(20)=3.1\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.2.2. </para><para>The correct answer is \(f(20)=3.1\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="42a31a54-653c-4215-b686-a6f8274741d7" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the value of \(f(140)\). </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(f(140)=3.5\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.2.2. </para><para>The correct answer is \(f(140)=3.5\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="908003dd-ec45-4360-990e-53b90e5315ba" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Looking at the graph below, what does the point \((1980, 83)\) mean?</para>
      <figure><media alt="GRAPH TITLED &quot;WORLD POPULATION INCREASE&quot; WITH X-AXIS LABEL OF YEAR AND Y-AXIS LABEL OF MILLIONS OF PEOPLE"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/40b276fc71442a0f5c1ecb39fb8c84f44669ed72"/></media></figure></problem><solution><para>Correct! In 1980, the world population increased by 80 million people.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.2.1 – 4.2.2. </para><para>The correct answer is: In 1980, the world population increased by 80 million people. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="0c5ed329-1ad3-4335-b6be-0b95861aeca4" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Function \(R\) gives the number of inches of rain after \(h\) hours. What does the statement \(R(6)=5\) mean?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! After 6 hours, there was 5 inches of rain.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.2.4.</para><para>The correct answer is after 6 hours, there was 5 inches of rain. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="4d8f6467-07db-4c6e-9a41-a64633c2c719" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Function \(R\) gives the number of inches, \(T\), of rain after \(h\), hours. Choose which function notation represents this situation.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(R=T(h)\)</para><para>\(h=R(T)\)</para><para>\(h=T(R)\)</para><para>\(T=R(h)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(T=R(h)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.2.4. </para><para>The correct answer is \(T=R(h)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="821b6bf9-1c0c-45ce-9ef8-cfa4c4e4bd45" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Use function notation to represent that the number of miles, \(m\), a car travels as a function of time, \(t\).</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(f=m(t)\)</para><para>\(m=f(t)\)</para><para>\(t=m(f)\)</para><para>\(t=f(m)\)</para></problem><solution><para> Correct! \(m=f(t)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.2.2 – 4.2.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(m=f(t)\). </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
